# Line widths: scale_linewidth

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

economics = Giggly.Datasets.economics()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`scale_linewidth()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/scale_linewidth.html), which covers
`scale_linewidth_binned()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's
code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names
what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

`scale_linewidth()` maps a continuous column to the width of lines and of polygons' outlines,
between a thinnest and a thickest width; `scale_linewidth_binned()` cuts the column into bins first.
The `size` aesthetic can still set a line's width, for historical reasons, but `linewidth` keeps the
width of a stroke apart from the size of an area. Giggly has
`Giggly.Plot.scale_linewidth_continuous/2` and `Giggly.Plot.scale_linewidth_discrete/2`, with their
`range:`, but not ggplot2's short name, `scale_linewidth()`, nor binned line width scales
(`scale_linewidth_binned()`), and by default maps `linewidth` to a column as ggplot2's default scale
does, and draws the legend that reads it back. A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG
document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines
`render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`. The data is
`Giggly.Datasets.economics/0`, ggplot2's `economics`: US economic time series, one row a month from
1967 to 2015, which the setup cell binds to `economics`, ggplot2's name for it.

## Line width from a column

```r
p <- ggplot(economics, aes(date, unemploy, linewidth = uempmed)) +
  geom_line(lineend = "round")
p
p + scale_linewidth("Duration of\nunemployment")
p + scale_linewidth(range = c(0, 4))
```

`uempmed` is the median number of weeks unemployed. A scale's first argument titles its legend, and
`range` sets the thinnest and the thickest widths. `scale_linewidth()` is
`Giggly.Plot.scale_linewidth_continuous/2`, its title `name:`.

```elixir
p =
  economics
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :date, y: :unemploy, linewidth: :uempmed})
  |> Plot.geom_line(lineend: :round)

render.(p)
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.scale_linewidth_continuous(name: "Duration of\nunemployment")
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.scale_linewidth_continuous(range: {0, 4})
|> render.()
```

## Binned widths

```r
# Binning can sometimes make it easier to match the scaled data to the legend
p + scale_linewidth_binned()
```

Not in Giggly yet: `scale_linewidth_binned()` and the legend that reads it back.
